# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values. Clerk and Liveblocks keys belong in
# `.env.local`:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_CLERK_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=pk_...
#   CLERK_SECRET_KEY=sk_...
#   LIVEBLOCKS_SECRET_KEY=sk_...   # https://liveblocks.io/dashboard/apikeys
#
# `LIVEBLOCKS_SECRET_KEY` is read only by `POST /api/liveblocks-auth`, which
# mints the room token the canvas connects with. `lib/liveblocks.ts` builds its
# client on first use rather than at import, so a missing key fails that one
# request instead of failing the build.

# Postgres connection string for the app and the Prisma CLI.
#
# Declare this in `.env` only. `prisma.config.ts` loads `.env` through
# `dotenv/config`; Next.js reads `.env` and `.env.local` and prefers
# `.env.local`. A second copy in `.env.local` means the app and
# `prisma migrate` can silently drift onto different databases.
#
# `sslmode=verify-full` is mandatory for any non-local host. The Prisma Console
# copy button gives you `?sslmode=require` — replace it. `require` currently
# emits a deprecation warning from pg-connection-string, and in pg v9 it drops
# to libpq semantics with no certificate or hostname verification, which leaves
# the connection open to man-in-the-middle. `lib/prisma.ts` throws at startup if
# a remote URL is missing `verify-full`.
#
# Local development against a Postgres without TLS (localhost, 127.0.0.1, ::1,
# host.docker.internal) is exempt from that check.
#
# A `prisma+postgres://` Accelerate URL is also accepted and bypasses the check
# entirely — `lib/prisma.ts` routes it through `accelerateUrl` instead of the
# `@prisma/adapter-pg` driver adapter.
DATABASE_URL="postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/DATABASE?sslmode=verify-full"
